  <static seed="1824" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicAlgebra/FractionArithmetic/MultiplyFractions40.pg">
    <statement><p>Multiply: <m>\displaystyle{-\frac{20}{3} \cdot \frac{11}{30}}</m></p>
<p><fillin characters="9" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-{\frac{22}{9}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>To multiply two fractions, multiply both numerators and both denominators. Don<rsq/>t forget to reduce the fraction if possible.</p>
<p>Remember: a positive number times a negative number is a negative number.</p>
<p>We have:</p>
<p><m>\displaystyle{\begin{aligned}
    -\frac{20}{3} \cdot \frac{11}{30}
    \amp =-\frac{2\cdot10}{3} \cdot \frac{11}{3\cdot10} \\
    \amp =-\frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{11}{3}\\ 
    \amp = -\frac{22}{9} 
    \end{aligned}}</m></p>
</solution>
